{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794044574,"nonce":"p0l1quI/vxOUm8B4CIEXJGZE1Jg=","title":"Collector: one all-live collection grants 4x the live set, doubling peak RSS","message":"A collection that frees under an eighth of what it reached grants a budget of 4x the live set (MAX_GROWTH in crates/compiler/src/collector.rs), so a program building a table is not re-marked at every doubling. When that collection lands at the peak of a sawtooth program, the grant overshoots: the garbage is about to die and the heap carries 4x of the peak.\n\nReproducer: a Lua loop that builds 300k strings into a list, joins them, and counts the tokens with gmatch, 40 times. Collections trace with ZYNTAX_TRACE_GC=1.\n\n- Normal run: one 4x grant, at the first collection with 27 MB live. Peak RSS 190 to 210 MB.\n- Bad run: a second 4x grant at a collection with 73 to 80 MB live (token list and text both live, 3 MB freed), budget 319 MB, heap held near 250 MB, peak RSS 330 to 385 MB.\n\nIt is not specific to a change that exposed it. A prototype that returned cached strings for pattern captures removed the count loop's garbage, so collections stopped landing there and fell into the build phase: 6 of 24 runs hit the second grant, against 0 of 24 for the build without it. The plain build never got the second grant across workload sizes 250k to 350k, but nothing in the policy prevents it.\n\nThe two kinds of program cannot be told apart at the collection: a table that keeps growing and a peak that is about to drop both free under an eighth.\n\nOptions, none measured on the GC benchmarks yet:\n- Cap the extra grant in absolute bytes (live + N MB instead of 4x live). Bounds the peak, costs more marks for large builders.\n- Use 4x only when live grew past the previous collection's by a factor and the previous one was also unproductive.\n- Leave it and treat the spread as the cost of fewer marks.\n\nDecide with the binary trees, large table build and zylua memory runs. Peak RSS here is bimodal, so compare many runs, not medians.\n\nThe capture cache itself (weak 4096-slot cache, SHARED header flag so automatic release leaves a shared string alone) cut instructions 8% and wall about 4% on this reproducer. That is too little to justify the SDK flag, so it was not committed.","files":null}]}